# Population density query
#
# Adapted from `population_query.pl` in the Ciao Prolog example collection,
# where it is one of the standard Prolog benchmarks.
#
# Two tables, a derived view, and a self-join with an arithmetic condition:
# the shape most real deductive-database queries have. Find every pair of
# countries whose population densities are within 5% of each other, with the
# denser one named first.
#
# Populations are in units of 100 000 people and areas in thousands of
# square miles, so density is scaled by 100 to keep it an integer; `/` on
# two integers truncates. The 5% band is written `20 * D1 < 21 * D2` rather
# than `D1 / D2 < 1.05` so the comparison stays in integer arithmetic.

input predicate population(country: string, people: integer).
input predicate area(country: string, sq_miles: integer).

# A refinement: a density is never negative. It is checked against the tuples
# derived, so on this data it passes silently.
#
# `--obligations` shows why checking and proving are different things, and
# `--verify` names the reason: `((D -1) (P -1) (A 51))`, a negative population.
# Nothing here says a population cannot be negative, so `P * 100 / A` need not be
# non-negative, and no solver will discharge the claim. The contract holds for
# this data and is not a theorem, which is exactly the gap between the two modes.
# Closing it needs the bound stated where the value enters, which for an input
# predicate this formulation has no place to put.
#
# Note which reasons are *not* in that counterexample. A zero area and an
# overflowing `P * 100` would each leave the division with no value, so the rule
# derives no tuple, so the contract says nothing about either. Those are
# hypotheses the encoder supplies rather than cases the claim has to survive.
density(C, D, _: D >= 0) :- population(C, P), area(C, A), D = P * 100 / A.

close_pair(C1, D1, C2, D2) :-
  density(C1, D1), density(C2, D2),
  D1 > D2,
  20 * D1 < 21 * D2.

?- close_pair(Denser, D1, Sparser, D2).

top(max(D)) :- density(_, D).
output predicate densest(C, D) :- density(C, D), top(D).
